      "text": "- why we still need agregation job here if we are using flink??",
      "fontSize": 16,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "- why we still need agregation job here if we are using flink??",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "created": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
    {
      "id": "1kZzlN4emV86xUUZ49oDh",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 19081.093319452353,
      "y": 12387.368093781592,
      "width": 994.3986206054688,
      "height": 120,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#2f9e44",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "hachure",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bCb",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 921643434,
      "version": 47,
      "versionNonce": 719818614,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1788591751421,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Yes, NoSQL databases can support transactions. The difference is that SQL databases typically provide broader\n ACID transactions across rows and tables, whereas NoSQL databases often limit transaction scope—for example,\n to a partition—or impose additional coordination costs for cross-partition transactions. So I wouldn't say \nNoSQL doesn't support transactions; I'd look at the guarantees and scope provided by the specific NoSQL database.\n\nhat coordination is expensive and can hurt scalability/availability.",
      "fontSize": 16,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Yes, NoSQL databases can support transactions. The difference is that SQL databases typically provide broader\n ACID transactions across rows and tables, whereas NoSQL databases often limit transaction scope—for example,\n to a partition—or impose additional coordination costs for cross-partition transactions. So I wouldn't say \nNoSQL doesn't support transactions; I'd look at the guarantees and scope provided by the specific NoSQL database.\n\nhat coordination is expensive and can hurt scalability/availability.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "created": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
    {
      "id": "PXtTAm0wgAp_Jn8zNCB3b",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 19064.11748208767,
      "y": 12338.73907173454,
      "width": 1064.798583984375,
      "height": 20,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#e03131",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "hachure",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bCc",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1222987882,
      "version": 166,
      "versionNonce": 1200638326,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1788591556182,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
