    },
    {
      "id": "uC6COID9DnrLfxoULpdm3",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 64099.17249072998,
      "y": 10876.180855657549,
      "width": 1632.399658203125,
      "height": 105,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "hachure",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bL9",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1482097546,
      "version": 7,
      "versionNonce": 314739670,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1788790907625,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "When they ask about scale or failures - Questions like \"what if a server crashes during processing?\" \nor \"how do you handle 10x traffic?\" are perfect openings to introduce async workers. \"With async workers, \nif one crashes mid-job, another worker picks it up from the queue. No user requests are lost.\"",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "When they ask about scale or failures - Questions like \"what if a server crashes during processing?\" \nor \"how do you handle 10x traffic?\" are perfect openings to introduce async workers. \"With async workers, \nif one crashes mid-job, another worker picks it up from the queue. No user requests are lost.\"",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "created": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
    {
      "id": "_YIJ40yySaNKt4HDeaVje",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 64092.76447301822,
      "y": 11126.093546416228,
      "width": 2125.19921875,
      "height": 210,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "hachure",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bLA",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1303075018,
      "version": 6,
      "versionNonce": 1200516810,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1788791761873,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "The interviewer probes: \"Some of your PDF reports take 5 seconds, but end-of-year reports take 5 hours. They're all in the same queue.\n What problems does this cause?\"\nLong jobs block short ones. A user requesting a simple report waits hours behind someone's massive year-end export. Worker utilization \nbecomes uneven as some workers process hundreds of quick jobs while others are stuck on single long jobs. Autoscaling becomes a huge pain \nwhen you can't predict how long jobs will take.\nThe solution is to separate queues by job type or expected duration. Quick reports go to a \"fast\" queue with many workers",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "The interviewer probes: \"Some of your PDF reports take 5 seconds, but end-of-year reports take 5 hours. They're all in the same queue.\n What problems does this cause?\"\nLong jobs block short ones. A user requesting a simple report waits hours behind someone's massive year-end export. Worker utilization \nbecomes uneven as some workers process hundreds of quick jobs while others are stuck on single long jobs. Autoscaling becomes a huge pain \nwhen you can't predict how long jobs will take.\nThe solution is to separate queues by job type or expected duration. Quick reports go to a \"fast\" queue with many workers",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "created": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
    {
      "id": "DZuUWZpt8oR2mmiiCJrhx",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 67437.07338461785,
      "y": 8895.509568005944,
      "width": 1432.199462890625,
      "height": 2730,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "hachure",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bLx",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 453924682,
      "version": 3,
      "versionNonce": 1017416906,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1788798510681,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How do we solve this?\n\nThere are 3 common approaches.\n\n1. Cache TTL\n\nYou tell CDN:\n\nCache this file for 1 hour.\n\nCDN\nlogo.png\nTTL = 1 hour\n\nAfter 1 hour, CDN considers the cached copy stale and fetches the latest version from origin.\n\nGood for files that don't change frequently.\n\n2. Cache invalidation/purge\n\nWhen you update the file:\n\nDeploy\n  ↓\nUpdate S3\n  ↓\nInvalidate CDN cache\n  ↓\nCDN removes old logo.png\n\nNext request:\n\nUser\n ↓\nCDN → cache miss\n ↓\nS3 → VERSION B\n ↓\nCDN caches VERSION B\n ↓\nUser\n\nThis is useful when you need changes to become visible immediately.\n\n3. ⭐ Versioned filenames — usually the best approach\n\nInstead of:\n\n/app.js\n\nyou deploy:\n\n/app.v1.js\n\nLater:\n\n/app.v2.js\n\nNow CDN can cache files for a very long time.\n\nCDN\n\n/app.v1.js → forever\n/app.v2.js → forever\n\nBecause when content changes, the URL changes.\n\nYour HTML changes from:\n\n<script src=\"/app.v1.js\"></script>\n\nto:\n\n<script src=\"/app.v2.js\"></script>\n\nThe browser requests a completely different URL, so there's no ambiguity.\n\nThis is called cache busting or content versioning.",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "How do we solve this?\n\nThere are 3 common approaches.\n\n1. Cache TTL\n\nYou tell CDN:\n\nCache this file for 1 hour.\n\nCDN\nlogo.png\nTTL = 1 hour\n\nAfter 1 hour, CDN considers the cached copy stale and fetches the latest version from origin.\n\nGood for files that don't change frequently.\n\n2. Cache invalidation/purge\n\nWhen you update the file:\n\nDeploy\n  ↓\nUpdate S3\n  ↓\nInvalidate CDN cache\n  ↓\nCDN removes old logo.png\n\nNext request:\n\nUser\n ↓\nCDN → cache miss\n ↓\nS3 → VERSION B\n ↓\nCDN caches VERSION B\n ↓\nUser\n\nThis is useful when you need changes to become visible immediately.\n\n3. ⭐ Versioned filenames — usually the best approach\n\nInstead of:\n\n/app.js\n\nyou deploy:\n\n/app.v1.js\n\nLater:\n\n/app.v2.js\n\nNow CDN can cache files for a very long time.\n\nCDN\n\n/app.v1.js → forever\n/app.v2.js → forever\n\nBecause when content changes, the URL changes.\n\nYour HTML changes from:\n\n<script src=\"/app.v1.js\"></script>\n\nto:\n\n<script src=\"/app.v2.js\"></script>\n\nThe browser requests a completely different URL, so there's no ambiguity.\n\nThis is called cache busting or content versioning.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "created": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
    {
      "id": "fNlm6_hrh5FssgTfK2ZXY",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 69412.3229628866,
      "y": 8803.972834689983,
      "width": 1770.99951171875,
      "height": 105,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "hachure",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bLy",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1023183114,
      "version": 47,
      "versionNonce": 659262730,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1788798526463,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Yes, CDN caching is configurable. We define cache rules based on URL path, HTTP method, headers, query parameters,\n and sometimes response headers. Static resources like images, JS and CSS are typically cached, while personalized \nor sensitive API responses are usually marked non-cacheable",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Yes, CDN caching is configurable. We define cache rules based on URL path, HTTP method, headers, query parameters,\n and sometimes response headers. Static resources like images, JS and CSS are typically cached, while personalized \nor sensitive API responses are usually marked non-cacheable",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "created": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
    {
      "id": "QAhxDTEsqo6aWRTcNwBwv",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 69424.70467986446,
      "y": 9163.446128117157,
      "width": 1709.3994140625,
      "height": 70,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "hachure",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bLz",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 640184650,
      "version": 4,
      "versionNonce": 1238149782,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1788798772695,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Use a signed CloudFront URL for frequently downloaded content. CloudFront checks access at an edge location and\n can serve a cached copy near the user, so your S3 bucket is only the origin when the edge cache misses.",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Use a signed CloudFront URL for frequently downloaded content. CloudFront checks access at an edge location and\n can serve a cached copy near the user, so your S3 bucket is only the origin when the edge cache misses.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "created": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
    {
      "id": "hxU6Zp4lLE7N_l5s4gx8O",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 69830.67966024984,
      "y": 10086.12200675471,
      "width": 4666.19580078125,
      "height": 175,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "hachure",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bM0",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 863997450,
      "version": 3,
      "versionNonce": 432477066,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1788801551656,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "\"We can move to a two-phase architecture with DynamoDB for persistent storage and SQS delay queues for precise execution timing.\n\nWhen a job is scheduled, we store it in DynamoDB. For jobs scheduled far in the future, they remain only in DynamoDB. A scheduler process runs every 5 minutes to scan DynamoDB for jobs coming up in the next time window (say, the next 10 minutes) and loads them into SQS with appropriate delivery delays.\n\nFor jobs that need to be executed soon (within the next 10 minutes), we'll add to DynamoDB but also directly insert them into SQS with the correct delay timeout. SQS's delivery delay feature ensures messages become visible precisely when needed, allowing us to meet our 2-second precision requirement.\"",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "\"We can move to a two-phase architecture with DynamoDB for persistent storage and SQS delay queues for precise execution timing.\n\nWhen a job is scheduled, we store it in DynamoDB. For jobs scheduled far in the future, they remain only in DynamoDB. A scheduler process runs every 5 minutes to scan DynamoDB for jobs coming up in the next time window (say, the next 10 minutes) and loads them into SQS with appropriate delivery delays.\n\nFor jobs that need to be executed soon (within the next 10 minutes), we'll add to DynamoDB but also directly insert them into SQS with the correct delay timeout. SQS's delivery delay feature ensures messages become visible precisely when needed, allowing us to meet our 2-second precision requirement.\"",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "created": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
    {
      "id": "fult5-Kz0gz-G2VZEVOIG",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 69760.67966024984,
      "y": 10596.12200675471,
      "width": 4435.1962890625,
      "height": 35,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "hachure",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bM1",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1185152650,
      "version": 3,
      "versionNonce": 1508467722,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1788801888373,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "You explained horizontal scaling well, but you did not cover fast elastic scaling behavior. A stronger answer would connect your workers to something like ECS or Kubernetes with scaling policies, or use a serverless model, so execution capacity grows quickly without manual intervention. ",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "You explained horizontal scaling well, but you did not cover fast elastic scaling behavior. A stronger answer would connect your workers to something like ECS or Kubernetes with scaling policies, or use a serverless model, so execution capacity grows quickly without manual intervention. ",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "created": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
    {
      "id": "kvNvNTRstaN4Vn0kbw_sY",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 69760.67966024984,
      "y": 10876.12200675471,
      "width": 3095.398193359375,
      "height": 140,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "hachure",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bM2",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 212031830,
      "version": 6,
      "versionNonce": 376619926,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1788802291256,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "\"We'll implement a two-pronged approach to handle both visible and invisible failures. For visible failures (like application errors), we'll use SQS's built-in retry mechanism with exponential backoff,\n configuring a maximum of 3 retries before moving to a status of \"FAILED\". For invisible failures (like worker crashes), we'll leverage SQS's visibility timeout feature. \nWe'll set a short visibility timeout (30 seconds) and have workers extend it periodically while processing longer jobs. This ensures fast failure detection while supporting\n jobs of any duration. Failed jobs are automatically made visible again for other workers to process, and after 3 failed attempts, they'll be moved to a \"FAILED\" stats in the database.\"",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "\"We'll implement a two-pronged approach to handle both visible and invisible failures. For visible failures (like application errors), we'll use SQS's built-in retry mechanism with exponential backoff,\n configuring a maximum of 3 retries before moving to a status of \"FAILED\". For invisible failures (like worker crashes), we'll leverage SQS's visibility timeout feature. \nWe'll set a short visibility timeout (30 seconds) and have workers extend it periodically while processing longer jobs. This ensures fast failure detection while supporting\n jobs of any duration. Failed jobs are automatically made visible again for other workers to process, and after 3 failed attempts, they'll be moved to a \"FAILED\" stats in the database.\"",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "created": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
    {
      "id": "OjXJ5a4muvYzdpUWydOWw",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 69657.30289269955,
      "y": 11516.234492204763,
      "width": 2664.19873046875,
      "height": 1645,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "hachure",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bM3",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 551635094,
      "version": 4,
      "versionNonce": 722690070,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1788802603262,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "So the flow is:\n\n              Queue\n                │\n        Receive Job A\n                │\n                ▼\n        Worker gets:\n        ┌─────────────────┐\n        │ Job A           │\n        │ ReceiptHandle   │\n        └─────────────────┘\n                │\n                │ every ~10-15 sec\n                ▼\n       ChangeVisibility()\n                │\n                ▼\n       timeout extended\n\nWhen the worker finishes:\n\nProcess Job A\n     ↓\nSUCCESS\n     ↓\nDeleteMessage(receiptHandle)\n\nIf the worker crashes:\n\nReceive Job A\n     ↓\nWorker crashes 💥\n     ↓\nNo more ChangeVisibility()\n     ↓\n30 sec timeout expires\n     ↓\nJob A becomes visible\n     ↓\nWorker 2 receives it\nFor an Amazon interview\n\nYou can say:\n\n\"The worker receives the message along with a receipt handle. While processing, it periodically calls the queue's visibility-extension API using that receipt handle.\n This acts as a heartbeat. If the worker crashes and stops extending the visibility timeout, the message eventually becomes visible again and another worker can process it.\"",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "So the flow is:\n\n              Queue\n                │\n        Receive Job A\n                │\n                ▼\n        Worker gets:\n        ┌─────────────────┐\n        │ Job A           │\n        │ ReceiptHandle   │\n        └─────────────────┘\n                │\n                │ every ~10-15 sec\n                ▼\n       ChangeVisibility()\n                │\n                ▼\n       timeout extended\n\nWhen the worker finishes:\n\nProcess Job A\n     ↓\nSUCCESS\n     ↓\nDeleteMessage(receiptHandle)\n\nIf the worker crashes:\n\nReceive Job A\n     ↓\nWorker crashes 💥\n     ↓\nNo more ChangeVisibility()\n     ↓\n30 sec timeout expires\n     ↓\nJob A becomes visible\n     ↓\nWorker 2 receives it\nFor an Amazon interview\n\nYou can say:\n\n\"The worker receives the message along with a receipt handle. While processing, it periodically calls the queue's visibility-extension API using that receipt handle.\n This acts as a heartbeat. If the worker crashes and stops extending the visibility timeout, the message eventually becomes visible again and another worker can process it.\"",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "created": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
    {
      "id": "zm0-Ws2ci_QZdeilFnKdr",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 70851.63540172153,
      "y": 11372.011016557415,
      "width": 831.5997314453125,
      "height": 420,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "hachure",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bM4",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1446252502,
      "version": 485,
      "versionNonce": 938724566,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1788802835387,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Visibility timeout :\n\n- Whenever the worker1 process the job A then \nfor 30 sec this job A become disappear for other\nworkers to consume\n\n- now if worker1 taking 5min then in 10-15sec, it kill\nupdating the SQS to extend timeout by 30 sec\n\n- but incase the worker1 failed, noboday will ask to \nupdate the timeout so job A, will be visible to other \nworker to consume",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Visibility timeout :\n\n- Whenever the worker1 process the job A then \nfor 30 sec this job A become disappear for other\nworkers to consume\n\n- now if worker1 taking 5min then in 10-15sec, it kill\nupdating the SQS to extend timeout by 30 sec\n\n- but incase the worker1 failed, noboday will ask to \nupdate the timeout so job A, will be visible to other \nworker to consume",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "created": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
    {
      "id": "fM0_ubi_RX9E-TdCH6iQT",
