      "updated": 1791638357813,
      "created": 1791638341813,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "what is Cross-Encoder Reranking (The Accuracy\nMultiplier)\n& why we use it?",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "what is Cross-Encoder Reranking (The Accuracy Multiplier)\n& why we use it?",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "euxmjwckFECQaDbq-M4Jm",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 71096.58868510279,
      "y": -3848.237839756829,
      "width": 2540.99853515625,
      "height": 910,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffc9c9",
      "fillStyle": "hachure",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bQk",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1674892808,
      "version": 37,
      "versionNonce": 112992008,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1791645014176,
      "created": 1791644872258,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "\n🛠️ The Architecture for Maximum Accuracy\n\nTo get the absolute highest accuracy for finding similar questions, combine three specific techniques:\n\n1. The Best Models & Dimensions\n\n    Do not use 3072 dimensions here. For question-to-question matching, 768 to 1024 dimensions provide the highest semantic density without adding noise.\n    • Open-Source Option: BAAI/bge-large-en-v1.5 (1024 dimensions) or sentence-transformers/all-mpnet-base-v2 (768 dimensions). These are explicitly fine-tuned \nfor sentence and question similarity.\n    • API Option: Cohere embed-english-v3.0 (1024 dimensions). Ensure you set the parameter input_type=\"search_query\" for both your database questions and the \nincoming user question.\n\n2. Vector Normalization + Cosine Similarity\n    \n    Always L2 normalize your embeddings before saving and searching. Because questions are short, variations in length (e.g., \"How to fix a flat tire?\" \nvs \"Could you please tell me how I can fix a flat tire on my car?\") can skew raw distance. Normalizing forces the system to look only at the angle (semantic meaning)\n rather than vector length.\n\n3. Cross-Encoder Reranking (The Accuracy Multiplier)\n\n    Embedding models (Bi-Encoders) compress questions into single vectors, which can miss tiny, critical logical differences (e.g., \"How do I import data?\" vs \n\"How do I export data?\" might look highly similar in embedding space).\n    1. Use your 1024-dimension embeddings to pull the top 20 most similar questions.\n    2. Pass those 20 candidates through a Cross-Encoder model (like BAAI/bge-reranker-large). The Cross-Encoder analyzes the two questions simultaneously,\n paying attention to precise word relationships, and guarantees the highest possible accuracy.",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "\n🛠️ The Architecture for Maximum Accuracy\n\nTo get the absolute highest accuracy for finding similar questions, combine three specific techniques:\n\n1. The Best Models & Dimensions\n\n    Do not use 3072 dimensions here. For question-to-question matching, 768 to 1024 dimensions provide the highest semantic density without adding noise.\n    • Open-Source Option: BAAI/bge-large-en-v1.5 (1024 dimensions) or sentence-transformers/all-mpnet-base-v2 (768 dimensions). These are explicitly fine-tuned \nfor sentence and question similarity.\n    • API Option: Cohere embed-english-v3.0 (1024 dimensions). Ensure you set the parameter input_type=\"search_query\" for both your database questions and the \nincoming user question.\n\n2. Vector Normalization + Cosine Similarity\n    \n    Always L2 normalize your embeddings before saving and searching. Because questions are short, variations in length (e.g., \"How to fix a flat tire?\" \nvs \"Could you please tell me how I can fix a flat tire on my car?\") can skew raw distance. Normalizing forces the system to look only at the angle (semantic meaning)\n rather than vector length.\n\n3. Cross-Encoder Reranking (The Accuracy Multiplier)\n\n    Embedding models (Bi-Encoders) compress questions into single vectors, which can miss tiny, critical logical differences (e.g., \"How do I import data?\" vs \n\"How do I export data?\" might look highly similar in embedding space).\n    1. Use your 1024-dimension embeddings to pull the top 20 most similar questions.\n    2. Pass those 20 candidates through a Cross-Encoder model (like BAAI/bge-reranker-large). The Cross-Encoder analyzes the two questions simultaneously,\n paying attention to precise word relationships, and guarantees the highest possible accuracy.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
